WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.659
A farmer has one field. She can grow carrots or potatoes. If she plants more carrots, there is less room for potatoes.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:21.403
Opportunity cost is the next best alternative given up. On a P P C, moving from A to B gives more carrots, but potatoes fall from eighty to sixty tonnes. Twenty tonnes of potatoes are given up.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:28.424
So the rule is: old amount of the other good, minus the new amount. Give the answer in units of that good.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:36.387
Step one, find the start point and read the other good. Step two, add the extra units, and read the other good again.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:44.363
Step three, subtract the new amount from the old amount, and write it out. Step four, name the good given up, with its units.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:59.731
Here is a real question from January 2023. Output is two hundred and fifty units of consumer goods. Calculate the opportunity cost of making an extra forty units of consumer goods. Two marks.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:07.275
Find the start. At two hundred and fifty consumer goods, the curve gives five hundred units of capital goods.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:17.675
Add forty: two hundred and fifty plus forty is two hundred and ninety. At two hundred and ninety consumer goods, the curve gives four hundred capital goods.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:33.579
Capital goods fall from five hundred to four hundred. Five hundred minus four hundred is one hundred. The opportunity cost is one hundred units of capital goods. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:42.573
A common mistake is subtracting the good that rises. The extra forty consumer goods are what you gain, not what you give up.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.957
Another is giving a bare number. Say which good, and use units.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:02:03.205
Now you try. On its P P C, a country making sixty cars can make ninety tractors. Making eighty cars, it can make seventy tractors. Calculate the opportunity cost of the extra twenty cars. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.821
Moving from sixty to eighty cars, tractors fall from ninety to seventy.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:14.157
Ninety minus seventy is twenty. The opportunity cost is twenty tractors.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:22.891
Remember: read the start, read the new point, subtract the good given up, and name it with units. Now try the practice questions.
